# dhcptest drives real DHCP transactions on the wire and asserts on the
# replies. It is implementation-agnostic: it never reads the xCAT database and
# never runs an xCAT command, so it tests what a booting node would actually
# see rather than what the generated config says.
#
# The first two cases are offline -- no network, no root, no scapy -- and are
# safe anywhere.
#
# The wire cases build their own provisioning network out of a veth pair, via
# dhcpfixture.sh, which is where all the xCAT knowledge lives: it defines a
# network with a dynamic range and one node with a MAC, runs makedhcp so the
# configuration is generated by xCAT rather than by hand, and puts everything
# back afterwards. That is what makes a wire test on a single-node management
# node -- the CI runner, above all -- mean something instead of quietly
# passing with nothing to talk to.
#
# Each wire case is one script rather than a series of commands, so its
# teardown runs from a trap and the machine is left serving its own config
# even when an assertion fails half way through.
#
# A case that cannot run says so and passes: no root, no scapy, no makedhcp,
# no veth support. Read a pass as real coverage only when the log shows the
# ok lines.

start:dhcptest_unit_tests
description:dhcptest's own unit tests: no network, no root, no scapy needed
label:mn_only,ci_test,dhcp
os:Linux
cmd:cd /opt/xcat/share/xcat/tools/autotest/dhcptest && python3 -m unittest discover -s tests
check:rc==0
end

